import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Cascadia Strategy for Beginners: Habitat Corridors vs Wildlife"
  description="New players chase animals and lose. Here's how to balance both halves of the scoresheet."
  game="cascadia"
  gameTitle="Cascadia"
  category="Strategy"
  product="cascadia-game-tray"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Almost everyone loses their first few games of Cascadia the same way: they
    play it as an animal game. The animals are the pretty part, the scoring cards
    are on the table demanding attention, and the terrain underneath feels like
    scenery. It isn't.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Choose your tile for the terrain, then find a home for the animal. Habitat corridors are quiet, reliable points that beginners give away for free." />

  <h2>The Two Scoresheets</h2>

  <p>
    Your final score comes from wildlife patterns, habitat corridors and leftover
    nature tokens. Wildlife is usually the larger number — but it's also the
    number every player at the table is fighting over, and it's the one that can
    collapse to nothing if your shapes don't come together.
  </p>

  <p>
    Habitat corridors are different. For each of the five terrains you score your
    largest connected group of that type, plus a bonus if it's the biggest at the
    table. These points arrive steadily and can't be taken away.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Corridors are about connection, not quantity">
    Ten scattered forest tiles score you the same as one forest tile. Only your
    single largest connected group counts, so a tile that <em>joins</em> two
    existing groups is worth several tiles that start new ones.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>A Better Turn Order in Your Head</h2>

  <StepList title="How to actually evaluate a turn">
    <Step number={1} title="Look at the terrain first">
      Which of the four tiles extends or connects a corridor you already care
      about? This narrows four options down to one or two immediately.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Check the paired animal">
      Can you place it usefully? Not just legally — usefully, in a spot that
      contributes to a pattern you're building.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Ask what it costs you">
      Does this tile block a line you were planning? A tile that helps one animal
      and wrecks another is often worse than the boring option.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Only then consider a nature token">
      If the best available pairing is still poor, that's what the pinecones are
      for. <a href="/games/cascadia/cascadia-nature-tokens-guide/">Spending them well</a> is a skill in itself.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <h2>Commit to Two Animals</h2>

  <p>
    You have twenty turns and five animals. You cannot score all of them well, and
    trying is the most common cause of a mediocre score.
  </p>

  <p>Pick two to genuinely build around, ideally one of each kind:</p>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-emerald-800 mb-3">🎯 One that needs planning</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-emerald-700/70">Elk or salmon. These need committed space and several turns of setup, so decide early and protect the room they need.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-sky-50 rounded-xl border border-sky-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-sky-800 mb-3">⚡ One that's opportunistic</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-sky-700/70">Fox or hawk. These reward reacting to whatever the display gives you, so they fill the turns where your main plan isn't being served.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Treat the remaining three as bonus points. If a bear pair falls into your lap,
    take it — but don't restructure your landscape for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Five Habits That Improve Your Score Immediately</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep your landscape compact.</strong> Sprawl looks impressive and scores badly. Tight landscapes have more adjacencies, which helps corridors and foxes simultaneously.</li>
    <li><strong>Leave deliberate gaps for bears.</strong> If you're scoring bear pairs, plan where the third bear <em>can't</em> go before you place the second.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't hoard pinecones.</strong> Spend early, keep late. One point is a poor return on a token that could have completed a corridor on turn four.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the largest-corridor bonuses.</strong> They're worth fighting for and they're visible — you can see exactly how big everyone's forest is.</li>
    <li><strong>Accept discarding an animal.</strong> Taking a great tile and binning the token is frequently the right play. Beginners take the bad tile to save the animal and lose points twice.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="The keystone trap">
    Single-symbol keystone tiles pay a nature token, which makes them feel
    valuable. But they're the most restrictive tiles in the game — only one animal
    will ever fit. Take them when the animal is one you're collecting, not because
    a free pinecone is a free pinecone.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Playing at Two vs Four</h2>

  <p>
    At two players, the largest-corridor bonuses are effectively a head-to-head
    duel and you can see exactly what your opponent is building — play more
    reactively, and contest whichever terrain they're clearly investing in.
  </p>

  <p>
    At four, the display turns over fast and you'll rarely get the tile you were
    hoping for. Plans need to be shorter and more flexible; committing to a
    five-long elk line at four players is usually wishful thinking.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="If you remember one thing">
    Terrain first, animal second. Every other habit on this page follows from
    that one.
  </InfoBox>
</Article>
